  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 0,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "doc_title",
          "index": 0,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Lithium-Ion Batteries: Chemistry, Materials and Performance"
            }
          ],
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A short technical overview prepared as a sample document for document parsing.",
              "styles": [
                "italic"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. History"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The first rechargeable lithium cell was built in the 1970s at "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exxon",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " by "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "M. Stanley Whittingham",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", who used a titanium disulfide cathode. In 1980 "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "John B. Goodenough",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " showed that lithium cobalt oxide gives a much higher voltage, and in 1985 "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Akira Yoshino",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " at Asahi Kasei replaced the reactive lithium metal anode with carbon. The three shared the "
            },
            {
              "type": "hyperlink",
              "url": "https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2019/summary/",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2019"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Lithium-ion batteries made portable electronics and modern electric vehicles possible by combining high energy density with long cycle life.",
              "styles": [
                "italic"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1.1 Key milestones"
            }
          ],
          "level": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Table 1. Milestones in lithium-ion battery technology",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "index": 7,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 7,
              "content": "<table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Year</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Event</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Organisation</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>People</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p>1976</p></td><td><p>Lithium metal / TiS₂ rechargeable cell</p></td><td><p>Exxon</p></td><td><p>M. S. Whittingham</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>1980</p></td><td><p>LiCoO₂ cathode proposed</p></td><td><p>University of Oxford</p></td><td><p>J. B. Goodenough</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>1985</p></td><td><p>Carbon anode, first practical Li-ion prototype</p></td><td><p>Asahi Kasei</p></td><td><p>A. Yoshino</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>1991</p></td><td><p>First commercial Li-ion cell</p></td><td><p>Sony</p></td><td><p>—</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>1997</p></td><td><p>LiFePO₄ (olivine) cathode reported</p></td><td><p>University of Texas at Austin</p></td><td><p>J. B. Goodenough, A. K. Padhi</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>2019</p></td><td><p>Nobel Prize in Chemistry</p></td><td><p>Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences</p></td><td><p>Goodenough, Whittingham, Yoshino</p></td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Electrochemistry"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2.1 Electrode reactions"
            }
          ],
          "level": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "During discharge, lithium ions leave the graphite anode and are inserted into the cathode. For a LiCoO₂ / graphite cell:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Cathode:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 12,
          "content": "Li_{1-x}CoO_{2} + xLi^{+} + xe^{-} \\rightleftharpoons LiCoO_{2}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Anode:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 14,
          "content": "Li_{x}C_{6} \\rightleftharpoons C_{6} + xLi^{+} + xe^{-}"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 15,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2.2 Capacity and energy"
            }
          ],
          "level": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The theoretical specific capacity of an electrode material follows from Faraday's law:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 17,
          "content": "Q_{th} = \\frac{nF}{3.6M}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "where:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "index": 19,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "- n — number of electrons transferred per formula unit;"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "- F — Faraday constant, 96 485 C/mol;"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "- M — molar mass of the active material, g/mol;"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "- the factor 3.6 converts C/g to mAh/g."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The energy delivered by a cell is the integral of voltage over the charge passed:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 21,
          "content": "E = \\int_{0}^{Q}V\\left(q\\right) dq \\approx V_{avg} \\cdot Q"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The charge or discharge current is often expressed as a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "C-rate",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ": at 1C a cell is fully discharged in one hour, at 2C in 30 minutes, and in general:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 23,
          "content": "I = C \\cdot Q_{nom},  t = \\frac{1}{C} h"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 24,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Cathode materials"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 25,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The cathode largely determines cell voltage, capacity, cost and safety. Cobalt-rich layered oxides offer high energy density, while LiFePO₄ trades capacity for thermal stability and cycle life."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 26,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Table 2. Common cathode materials (approximate values)",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "index": 27,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 27,
              "content": "<table><thead><tr><th rowspan=\"2\"><p><strong>Structure</strong></p></th><th rowspan=\"2\"><p><strong>Material</strong></p></th><th rowspan=\"2\"><p><strong>Nominal cell voltage, V</strong></p></th><th colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>Specific capacity, mAh/g</strong></p></th><th rowspan=\"2\"><p><strong>Typical applications</strong></p></th></tr><tr><th><p><strong>Theoretical</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Practical</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan=\"2\"><p>Layered oxide</p></td><td><p>LiCoO₂ (LCO)</p></td><td><p>3.7</p></td><td><p>274</p></td><td><p>≈ 150</p></td><td><p>Phones, laptops</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>NMC811</p></td><td><p>3.7</p></td><td><p>≈ 275</p></td><td><p>≈ 200</p></td><td><p>Electric vehicles</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Spinel</p></td><td><p>LiMn₂O₄ (LMO)</p></td><td><p>3.7</p></td><td><p>148</p></td><td><p>≈ 110</p></td><td><p>Power tools</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Olivine</p></td><td><p>LiFePO₄ (LFP)</p></td><td><p>3.2</p></td><td><p>170</p></td><td><p>≈ 160</p></td><td><p>EVs, stationary storage</p></td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 28,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 28,
              "image_path": "images/page_0_image_28.png",
              "content": "Theoretical and practical specific capacity of cathode materials"
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 29,
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Figure 1. Theoretical vs practical specific capacity of cathode materials.",
                  "styles": [
                    "italic"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 30,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Computing theoretical capacity"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 31,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The formula from Section 2.2 in Python:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 32,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "F = 96485.0  # Faraday constant, C/mol"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 33,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "def theoretical_capacity(molar_mass: float, n: int = 1) -> float:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 34,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "return n * F / (3.6 * molar_mass)  # mAh/g"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 35,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "print(f\"LiFePO4: {theoretical_capacity(157.76):.1f} mAh/g\")  # 169.9 mAh/g"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 36,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Summary"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "index": 37,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "1. Lithium-ion technology was developed between 1976 and 1991 and commercialised by Sony."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "2. A cell consists of:"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "content": "- a graphite anode;"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "content": "- a transition-metal oxide or phosphate cathode;"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "content": "- a liquid electrolyte with a lithium salt, usually LiPF₆."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "3. Cathode choice trades off energy density, cost, safety and cycle life."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 38,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Glossary"
            }
          ],
          "level": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 39,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Specific capacity",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " — charge stored per unit mass of active material, mAh/g."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 40,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Intercalation",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " — reversible insertion of ions into a host crystal structure without destroying it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 41,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "SEI",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " — solid electrolyte interphase, a passivation layer formed on the anode during the first cycles."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
